{{smaller|'''Ventilated bucket,''' a bucket in a water-wheel having provision for the escape of the air carried into it by the water.}}

'''bucket''' (buk′et), ''v.'' [< ''bucket, n.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To dip up water with a bucket; use a bucket.
{{smaller block|<poem>Like Danaides’ Sieve-like Tub is filling ever,
But never full for all their ''bucketing''.</poem>
{{right|''Sylvester'', Memorials of Mortalitie, st. 23.}}}}
'''2.''' [In allusion to the rapid motion of a bucket in a well.] To move fast. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|He sprang into the saddle smiling, because the visit was over, and ''bucketed'' back at a hand-gallop. ''Dickens.''}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To pour water upon with a bucket.
{{smaller block|Wo be to him whose head is ''bucketed'' with waters of a scalding bath. ''Bp. Hacket'', Life of Abp. Williams, ii. 194.}}

'''bucket-engine''' (buk′et-en″jin), ''n.'' An application of the principle of the water-wheel, consisting of a series of buckets attached to an endless chain which runs over a pair of sprocket-wheels, from either one or both of which power may be obtained: designed to utilize a stream of water which has a considerable fall, but is limited in quantity. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''bucketful''' (buk′et-fu̇l), ''n.'' [< ''bucket'' + ''-ful.'']

