{{smaller block|The top-mast studding-sail boom, after ''buckling'' up and springing out again like a piece of whalebone, broke off at the boom-iron. ''R. H. Dana, Jr.'', Before the Mast, p. 76.}}
'''2.''' To curl; become wrinkled; shrivel up.
{{smaller block|Melted and ''buckled'' with the heat of the fire like parchment. {{right|''Pepys'', Diary, Sept. 5, 1666.}}}}
'''3.''' To yield assent; agree: with ''to'': as, I can’t ''buckle to'' that. [Colloq.]—'''4.''' To bend to something; apply one’s self with vigor; engage in with zeal: with ''to'': as, “go, ''buckle to'' the law,” ''Dryden''.—'''5.''' To enter upon some labor or contest; struggle; contend: with ''with''.
{{smaller block|The bishop was as able and ready to ''buckle with'' the lord protector, as he was ''with'' him.
{{right|''Latimer'', 2d Serm. bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Why met you not the Tartar, and defied him?
Drew your dead-doing sword, and ''buckled with'' him?</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Loyal Subject, iv. 5.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To buckle in''', to close in; embrace or seize the body, as in a scuffle. [U. S.]}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To bend; curl; shrivel as by the application of heat.
{{smaller block|<poem>Like a bow ''buckled'' and bent together
By some more strong in mischiefs than myself.</poem>
{{right|''Ford and Dekker'', Witch of Edmonton, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|The force generated in these plates [accumulator], tending to ''buckle'' them, is something enormous, and no ordinary separator would be able to resist this. ''Science'', IV. 389.}}

